{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>Take a goat for a sin-offering, and a calf and a lamb, yearlings without blemish, for a burnt-offering.</b> They said in the Midrash: What did Israel see to bring more than Aaron? Rather, you have it in your hand at the beginning and you have it in your hand at the end — at the beginning (Genesis 37:31): ״וישחטו שעיר עזים״, and at the end (Exodus 32:8): ״עשו להם עגל מסכה״. Let the goat come and atone for the deed of the goat; let the calf come and atone for the deed of the calf." "<b>And it appears</b> to me that the reason He commanded Aaron that his calf be a sin-offering, and commanded concerning that of Israel that it be a burnt-offering, is so that the merit of Aaron's heart would be made clear from this: that even though he made the calf with his own hand, he did not sin in it in his thought at all, but his intention was for the sake of Heaven. And so it appears to me from what the verse adds to say (Exodus 32:35): ״אשר עשה אהרן״ — for certainly this had already been specified when it says (Exodus 32:4): ״ויקח מידם ויצר אותו בחרט ויעשהו עגל מסכה״, so why was ״אשר עשה אהרן״ necessary? Rather, it comes to say that Scripture holds him accountable only for the deed alone and not for the thought — it is as though it says explicitly that he sinned in deed and not in thought. And because of this He had him bring a sin-offering, for the sin-offering comes for sin in deed, whereas for that of Israel He commanded it to be a burnt-offering, for the burnt-offering comes for the straying of the heart. And it is known that Israel thought an evil thought concerning it, and behold their sin in it in thought was a deed."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:24:14.220242004Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}